ay - Colonial violence was frequently framed in religious and civilizational terms - Christian just war theory has been used to justify wars for centuries The pattern isn't "some cultures uniquely justify violence religiously" — it's that **any large, old religious or cultural tradition can be selectively interpreted to justify violence by a minority faction**, while being interpreted by the majority to oppose it. Christianity, Islam, Judaism, Hinduism, Buddhism — all have had violent extremist offshoots that mainstream adherents reject. ## Why Poverty/War Aren't "Noise" They're not noise — they're the **variable that actually predicts when and where violence escalates**, even holding religion/culture constant. Yemen had the same tribal structure and same dominant religion before 2011 and after — what changed dramatically was governance collapse and civil war, and that's exactly when AQAP's territorial control and recruitment spiked. If the "code" were the core driver, you'd expect a constant, not a spike correlated with war and economic collapse. ## Where This Leaves Us I'm not going to tell you something is well-supported when it isn't, even if it lines up with a comfortable, simple narrative. The evidence points clearly to conflict/governance conditions as the driver, with religious/tribal identity being incidental context, not causal. That's not political correctness — it's just what actually explains the variation in violence over time and place.
