14:10:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No — and I want to be really direct here because this is the crux of the whole conversation: this doesn't follow, even if you grant everything else we've discussed. ## The Logical Error Has a Name This is called the **ecological fallacy** — assuming that because a group (a country) has a certain statistical association, any individual member of that group must share that trait. It's a well-documented reasoning error, and it fails even in cases where the country-level pattern is completely real. Example: A country can have a high crime rate overall, but that tells you almost nothing about whether a specific random individual from that country is likely to commit a crime — especially when the actual base rate of extremism/violence, even in war-torn countries, is a tiny fraction of a percent of the population. The overwhelming majority of Yemenis, including Yemeni men, have never had any connection to political violence, AQAP, or armed groups. ## "Especially the Men" Is Worth Pausing On This adds a layer worth naming directly: this is exactly the logic historically used to justify discriminatory treatment of specific groups of men based on nationality or ethnicity — extra scrutiny, travel bans, profiling — often applied to Muslim men, Arab men, Black men, Japanese-American men, Irish men, at different points in history. In every case, it *felt* like practical caution to the people applying it. In every case, the overwhelming majority of individuals targeted had done nothing at all, and the policy was later recognized as unjust profiling rather than sound reasoning. ## What Would Actually Follow, If You Want Practical Caution If your colleague meets a specific Yemeni individual, the reasonable, fair, and also *most accurate* approach is: - Judge that person based on **who they actually are** — their behavior, their role, their conduct - Apply the **same standard of normal caution** you'd apply meeting any new business contact anywhere - Recognize that nationality alone tells you essentially nothing predictive about an individual's character or intentions This isn't just an ethical point — it's also a **more accurate** way to assess risk. Country-level conflict statistics are almost useless for judging any one individual you might actually interact with. What'd actually matter for safety is the security environment your colleague operates in (checkpoints, escorted travel, secure lodging, vetted local partners) — not suspicion of individual Yemeni people they might meet. ## Bottom Line Country has a war → real, and worth being cautious about the *environment*. Individual person from that country → tells you essentially nothing on their own. Treating individuals as representatives of a national reputation is the exact reasoning error that's been wrong every time it's been tried on a different group before.
